Can you explain how to implement partitioning in Azure Event Hubs and its impact on performance?

Partitioning in Azure Event Hubs is used to scale event ingestion and processing.
An Event Hub is divided into multiple partitions, and each partition acts as an independent ordered log of events.
When producers send events, they can specify a partition key such as a device ID or customer ID. Events with the same partition key are always routed to the same partition, which preserves ordering.
Partitions enable parallel processing because different consumers within a consumer group can process different partitions simultaneously.
Increasing the number of partitions improves throughput and scalability, but it also requires more consumer instances to fully utilize the parallelism.
If no partition key is provided, Event Hubs distributes events across partitions using round-robin balancing.
One important design consideration is that partition count cannot be changed later in the Standard tier, so it should be selected based on expected future scale and downstream processing requirements.
